tgs:Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Nora Bateson: \"Complexity Between the Lines\""@en ;
    skos:definition "Nora's first TGS appearance, bringing systems/complexity thinking to bear on the show's usual physical-science framing — how ecosystems (and human systems) develop through mutual learning (symmathesy), how relationships escalate through cumulative interaction (schismogenesis), and what her own concept of Warm Data offers as a way of perceiving the relational context conventional data strips away."@en ;
    dct:created "2022-02-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2022-03-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NoraBateson ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/10-nora-bateson> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/TGS10NoraBatesonTranscript.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.WarmData, tgs:Concept.Symmathesy, tgs:Concept.Schismogenesis, tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.KleibersLaw ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.RealityBlind, tgs:Organization.InternationalBatesonInstitute ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: fully fleshed out from a direct real fetch of the episode's own page (MJSullivan's own request), confirming every one of his manually-transcribed timestamp labels against the page's real embedded links. Real, further citable material found but NOT built into this graph in this pass: William Bateson (Nora's grandfather, a real historical geneticist, distinct from her father Gregory already in this graph), Warm Data's own dedicated site (warmdatalab.net, distinct from International Bateson Institute's own site already used elsewhere), and 'People Need People' (peopleneedpeople.online) — real but unclear enough in nature (organization vs. informal initiative) from this one citation alone to model confidently. The Superorganism citation here is notably a DIFFERENT, real external ScienceDirect paper, not Nate's own writing — consistent with (and further corroborating) this graph's own earlier classification of Concept.Superorganism as external-origination, not thinkr:HagensConcept."@en ;
    .

