#############################################

tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_5_FoodAndCommunityInTheRuins a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.PanelDiscussion ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.RealityRoundtable ;
    skos:prefLabel "Food & Community in the Ruins: Dougald Hine, Chris Smaje, Pella Thiel"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate brings together three first-time guests, already personal friends by the time of recording — social thinker Dougald Hine (Dark Mountain Project, 'At Work in the Ruins'), small-farm writer Chris Smaje ('A Small Farm Future'), and Swedish ecologist/activist Pella Thiel (Transition Network) — to explore what rebuilding local food systems and genuine community actually looks like on the way toward a lower-energy, more localized future, organized around Hine's own framing of finding worthwhile 'work in the ruins' of a world that is ending, without mistaking that ending for the end of the world entirely."@en ;
    dct:created "2023-10-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2023-10-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.DougaldHine, tgs:Persona.ChrisSmaje, tgs:Persona.PellaThiel ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/rr05-hine-smaje-thiel> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/RR05Transcript.docx.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti ;
    skos:scopeNote "This episode's own real reference to 'Vanessa Machado de Oliveira' and her book 'Hospicing Modernity' is the same real person already built in this graph as tgs:Persona.VanessaAndreotti (her full/maiden name) — not a new individual. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Pella Thiel's own real later solo TGS episode (#121); Federico Campagna, Paul Richards, Ivan Illich, and Ed Gillespie are all real, directly cited thinkers/collaborators not bootstrapped here."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Uniformly warm and complementary, distinct in one real way from most other built RRs: the three guests were already personal friends before this recording (Nate had stayed at Pella Thiel's farm helping with lambing the night before, and had met both her and Hine in person in Sweden weeks earlier), so the conversation reads less like three experts meeting for the first time and more like a continuation of an existing relationship. The three framings are genuinely complementary rather than competing: Hine supplies the mythic/narrative register (his own 'at work in the ruins' framing, by way of the Italian philosopher Federico Campagna), Smaje supplies grounded agrarian-political realism (skeptical of both techno-fix optimism and any guru promising shortcuts, insistent on the specific, unglamorous politics of land access), and Thiel supplies lived, embodied community organizing (the real 'Potato Appeal' campaign she ran during COVID, modeled directly on a real 1917 Swedish uprising). The one small moment of real, friendly cross-checking rather than disagreement: Thiel attributes an idea to 'Paul Kingsnorth,' and Hine gently corrects her in real time ('It's one of his neighbors. It's Mark Boyle') — the kind of small factual collaboration that happens between people who already know each other well, not a substantive disagreement. Nate's own asides are genuinely personal throughout (his own visceral reaction to the guests' articulateness, his own coined phrase 'pro-social prepping'), and the episode closes on an unusually tender note — Hine speaking directly about his own son and the conversations he has with people weighing whether to become parents 'in a time like this.'"@en ;
    .

